    "lawSection": "Public Authorities",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S5570\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\nAn act\nin relation to the designation of technology development centers and to\nprovide funding for such centers\n\nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\nThis legislation extends the\ndesignation of entities as regional technology development centers.\n\nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\nSection 1 provides that entities\ndesignated as regional technology development centers as of April 1,\n2011 shall remain designated as such through at least March 31, 2012\nand that contracts in effect as of April 1, 2011 shall be deemed\ndesignated through at least March 31, 2012. The section also\nestablishes that funding shall not be less than the amount received\nin the previous state fiscal year of 2010-2011.\n\nSection 2 establishes an immediate effective date.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\nThe Regional Technology Development centers/Technology\nDevelopment organizations (RTDCS/TDOS) are designated for five year\nperiods. The current designation for the ten centers will expire on\nJune 30, 2011. The designations are done through a competitive\nprocess that has not yet been initiated. It will be very difficult to\ncomplete the competitive reprocess within the necessary timeframe.\nwithout formal designation/contract, the RTDCs/TDOs will not be able\nto receive federal funds to help fulfill their mission. This\nlegislation will simply extend the current designation.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\nNone.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\nThe TDO program and the related Industrial\nTechnology Extension Service (ITES) (administered by the RTDCs) have\nalready been appropriated funds in the SFY 2011-12 enacted budget to\nthe amounts of $1,312,000 for the TDO program and $921,000 for ITES.\nThis bill would have no additional fiscal impact.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately,\n\n",
